+
+      <span class="blog-tag">Announcing</span>
+      <h1 class="blog-title">Introducing ForkMesh: code that can't be taken down</h1>
+      <div class="blog-meta">
+        <span>The ForkMesh team</span>
+        <span class="dot"></span>
+        <time datetime="2026-06-23">June 23, 2026</time>
+        <span class="dot"></span>
+        <span>6 min read</span>
+      </div>
+
+      <img class="blog-hero-img" src="/blog/assets/introducing-forkmesh.svg"
+           alt="A distributed mesh of Git repository nodes connected through encrypted relays, with the ForkMesh wordmark.">
+
+      <p class="blog-lede">
+        Every line of code you've ever pushed lives on someone else's server, behind
+        someone else's terms of service, one policy change away from disappearing.
+        ForkMesh exists to end that. It's a distributed Git network where your code
+        stays on <em>your</em> machine, every action is signed with <em>your</em> key,
+        and the people who keep repositories alive actually get paid for it.
+      </p>
+
+      <div class="blog-body">
+        <p>
+          Today we're opening up ForkMesh — an open-source desktop node and relay for
+          hosting, mirroring, discovering, and discussing software projects without
+          depending on a single code-hosting company. It is not a GitHub skin and it
+          is not a blockchain repo. It's a different shape entirely: a mesh of peers
+          where the network coordinates, but no one in the middle holds your code
+          hostage.
+        </p>
+
+        <hr class="blog-divider">
+
+        <h2>Why we built it</h2>
+        <p>
+          Centralized forges have been extraordinary for collaboration and catastrophic
+          for resilience. Accounts get suspended. Projects get DMCA'd into the void.
+          Whole communities vanish when a company pivots, gets acquired, or simply
+          decides your repository is no longer welcome. The code didn't break any laws
+          of physics — it was just stored in exactly one place that someone else
+          controlled.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          ForkMesh starts from a stubborn premise: <strong>the canonical copy of your
+          code is the bare Git mirror on your own disk.</strong> The network's job is to
+          help people find it, clone it, talk about it, and mirror it — never to be the
+          single warehouse that can be raided or switched off.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2>The revolutionary part</h2>
+        <p>
+          Six ideas make ForkMesh feel fundamentally different the moment you use it.
+        </p>
+
+        <div class="feature-grid">
+          <div class="feature-card">
+            <div class="ico" style="background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent-bright) 18%,transparent)">🗄️</div>
+            <h4>Your machine is the server</h4>
+            <p>The relay is a control plane, not storage. Git objects stream from a connected host node — there is no central repo warehouse to seize.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="feature-card">
+            <div class="ico" style="background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--primary) 18%,transparent)">🔑</div>
+            <h4>Keys, not passwords</h4>
+            <p>Your identity is a local Ed25519 keypair. Profiles, issues, and pull requests are cryptographically signed — portable across any relay.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="feature-card">
+            <div class="ico" style="background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--purple) 22%,transparent)">🔒</div>
+            <h4>Chat the relay can't read</h4>
+            <p>Per-repository rooms are encrypted client-side with AES-256-GCM. The relay only ever sees ciphertext envelopes it forwards.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="feature-card">
+            <div class="ico" style="background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--warning) 22%,transparent)">◎</div>
+            <h4>Get paid to mirror</h4>
+            <p>Profiles and repos carry Solana addresses. Host a mirror, earn payouts; merged bounty PRs split the reward to the contributor automatically.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="feature-card">
+            <div class="ico" style="background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent-bright) 18%,transparent)">🌐</div>
+            <h4>Federated by design</h4>
+            <p>Anyone can run a relay node, Matrix- and Mastodon-style. No company sits at the center of the network — or owns its leaderboards.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="feature-card">
+            <div class="ico" style="background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--primary) 18%,transparent)">⑂</div>
+            <h4>Signed history in Git</h4>
+            <p>Issues and PRs are append-only signed events that can live as files inside the repository itself — your project's history travels with the code.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <h2><span class="num">01</span>Your machine is the server</h2>
+        <p>
+          When you publish a repository, ForkMesh doesn't upload your packfiles anywhere.
+          Your desktop node opens an outbound encrypted tunnel to the relay, and clone
+          and file-browse requests are routed back through that tunnel to your local
+          mirror on demand. Clone the canonical project exactly the way you'd expect:
+        </p>
+        <pre><code>git clone https://forkmesh.com/mainnode/forkmesh</code></pre>
+        <p>
+          The relay never becomes the source of truth. If it vanished tomorrow, every
+          repository would still exist — fully intact — on the machines that host them.
+          That's the whole point.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2><span class="num">02</span>Identity you own</h2>
+        <p>
+          There's no email-and-password gate to start. The desktop node generates a local
+          Ed25519 identity for you, and that key signs your profile, your repository
+          metadata, your issues, and your pull requests. Because identity is a key rather
+          than a row in a company's database, it's yours to carry to any relay in the
+          network.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2><span class="num">03</span>Conversations the relay can't read</h2>
+        <p>
+          Every repository gets its own encrypted room. Messages are sealed in your
+          client with AES-256-GCM before they ever touch the wire; the relay's Durable
+          Object forwards opaque ciphertext to connected peers and persists nothing.
+          Coordination, maintainer chatter, release talk — all of it stays between the
+          people holding the room key.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2><span class="num">04</span>A network that pays its hosts</h2>
+        <p>
+          Mirroring is the act that keeps open source alive, and ForkMesh treats it as
+          first-class work. Profiles and repositories publish Solana addresses, hosts
+          accrue payouts for the uptime they contribute, and repository bounties settle
+          on a merged pull request — splitting the reward to the contributor who did the
+          work. Keeping code alive stops being a favor and starts being something the
+          network rewards.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2><span class="num">05</span>Signed issues and pull requests</h2>
+        <p>
+          Collaboration history shouldn't live in a proprietary silo either. ForkMesh
+          issues and PRs are signed, append-only events that can be represented as files
+          inside the repository:
+        </p>
+        <pre><code>forkmesh-pull-event-v1
+&lt;author pubkey&gt;
+&lt;ts&gt;
+sha256hex(title + NUL + base + NUL + head + NUL + patch)</code></pre>
+        <p>
+          When a maintainer merges, the accepted record becomes part of normal Git
+          history — so the conversation around your code travels with the code, forever.
+        </p>
+
+        <h2><span class="num">06</span>No center to capture</h2>
+        <p>
+          Relays are federated. The hosted <code>mainnode</code> offers conveniences —
+          repository catalogs, encrypted rooms, mirror-health indexing, Solana metadata,
+          leaderboards — but it owns none of them by right. Anyone can run their own. The
+          network is a mesh, not a hub, and that's exactly what makes it hard to take
+          down.
+        </p>
+
+        <blockquote>
+          ForkMesh is a Git app first. Chat, payments, and discovery exist to support
+          repositories, mirrors, and the maintainers who keep them alive.
+        </blockquote>
+
+        <h2>This is an MVP — and that's exciting</h2>
+        <p>
+          We'll be honest about where we are: ForkMesh is an early prototype. Live file
+          browsing and clone need at least one host online. <code>git push</code> over the
+          relay isn't production-ready yet, and pull requests today are signed patch
+          records rather than full branch refs. The relay is still a trusted jump layer
+          for connection metadata.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          But the foundation that matters is already standing: real Ed25519 identity, real
+          Git mirroring, real encrypted rooms, real Solana payouts, and a real federated
+          relay. Everything from here is building outward from a base that, by design,
+          can't be quietly switched off.
+        </p>
+
+        <div class="blog-cta">
+          <h2>Run a node. Mirror a repo. Get paid.</h2>
+          <p>The desktop node starts with no setup and connects to the network out of the box.</p>
+          <div class="row">
+            <a class="primary-button" href="/#install-banner">Install ForkMesh</a>
+            <a class="secondary-button" href="/docs/">Read the docs</a>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </main>
+  </body>
+</html>
