+
+    <main class="about-wrap">
+      <p class="eyebrow">Our story</p>
+      <h1>Code hosting that can't be taken away.</h1>
+      <p class="about-lede">
+        ForkMesh began with a loss — and a refusal to let it happen again.
+      </p>
+
+      <h2>The day the code disappeared</h2>
+      <p>
+        One ordinary morning, <strong>Md Kaif Ansari</strong> opened GitHub to
+        find his repositories gone. No warning he could act on, no second copy to
+        fall back on. Years of work — side projects, experiments, the small tools
+        he'd quietly built for himself and others — vanished behind someone
+        else's policy and someone else's servers.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        The code wasn't deleted because it was wrong. It was deleted because it
+        lived in exactly one place, and that place answered to no one but itself.
+      </p>
+
+      <blockquote class="pullquote">
+        “If a single account can erase years of work overnight, then we never
+        really owned our code — we were only borrowing it.”
+      </blockquote>
+
+      <h2>So he built the antidote</h2>
+      <p>
+        ForkMesh is what Kaif wished he'd had that morning: a network where your
+        code doesn't sit on one company's servers, waiting to be locked out. Every
+        repository lives on real machines run by real people — mirrored, signed,
+        and streamed peer-to-peer. No central forge holds your objects hostage.
+        Take one node offline and the mesh carries on.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        Around that foundation grew everything a modern project needs: issues and
+        milestones, pull requests and releases, encrypted chat, real CI, and AI
+        agents that can pick up an issue and ship a fix — all without surrendering
+        your work to a single point of failure. And because the people who keep
+        the network alive deserve to be paid, ForkMesh has rewards and bounties
+        built right in.
+      </p>
+
+      <h2>Meet the founders</h2>
+      <div class="team">
+        <article class="member">
+          <div class="member-head">
+            <img class="member-photo" src="/assets/kaif.webp" alt="Md Kaif Ansari" width="76" height="76" />
+            <div>
+              <div class="member-name">Md Kaif Ansari</div>
+              <div class="member-role">Founder &amp; Full-Stack Developer</div>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <p class="member-bio">
+            Md Kaif Ansari is a full-stack developer from India with a passion for
+            building resilient, well-crafted software — and for taking things
+            apart to understand how to make them better. He works primarily across
+            the MERN stack and currently builds full-stack products for an
+            Australian startup.
+          </p>
+          <p class="member-bio">
+            Beyond his day-to-day work, Kaif is an active open-source contributor
+            drawn to projects with real-world impact, and a dedicated Python and
+            AI enthusiast who treats every hard problem as a puzzle worth solving.
+            A regular on the hackathon circuit, he thrives in fast-paced
+            environments where ambitious ideas become working products — and he
+            approaches every day as a chance to learn and push his craft further.
+          </p>
+        </article>
+
+        <article class="member">
+          <div class="member-head">
+            <img class="member-photo" src="/assets/jett.webp" alt="Jett Vale" width="76" height="76" />
+            <div>
+              <div class="member-name">Jett Vale</div>
+              <div class="member-role">Co-Founder &amp; Systems Engineer</div>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <p class="member-bio">
+            Jett Vale is a developer and Kaif's partner in building ForkMesh, with
+            a deep love for the layer most people never see — distributed systems,
+            peer-to-peer networking, and the quiet infrastructure that keeps data
+            alive. He cut his teeth writing performance-critical backends in Rust
+            and Go, and is happiest tracing a packet through a mesh until it does
+            exactly what it should.
+          </p>
+          <p class="member-bio">
+            When a deleted repository turned into a mission, Jett was the one
+            mapping out how code could live everywhere at once instead of nowhere.
+            He's a tinkerer and an open-source believer who cares as much about
+            clean protocols as he does about the people relying on them — and he's
+            convinced the best software is the kind that simply refuses to
+            disappear.
+          </p>
+        </article>
+      </div>
+
+      <h2>Where we're headed</h2>
+      <p>
+        Source code is infrastructure. It shouldn't depend on the goodwill of a
+        single host. ForkMesh exists so that the next developer who opens their
+        account to find it empty has somewhere else their work already lives.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        We're just getting started — and we'd love to hear from you. Reach the
+        founders directly at
